.\" openi2pd: native auxiliary tools reference.
.\"
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt OPENI2PD-TOOLS 1
.Os openi2pd
.Sh NAME
.Nm openi2pd-tools ,
.Nm autoconf_i2pd ,
.Nm b33address ,
.Nm b33offlinekeys ,
.Nm famtool ,
.Nm i2pbase64 ,
.Nm keygen ,
.Nm keyinfo ,
.Nm offlinekeys ,
.Nm regaddr ,
.Nm regaddr_3ld ,
.Nm regaddralias ,
.Nm routerinfo ,
.Nm vain ,
.Nm verifyhost ,
.Nm x25519
.Nd native I2P configuration, identity and RouterInfo tools
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These commands use the native openi2pd libraries for I2P configuration,
destination keys, host registration and RouterInfo inspection.
They are separate programs; the
.Nm openi2pd-tools
name identifies this manual page, not an executable.
No router needs to be running for the operations described here.
.Pp
Operands named
.Ar keys
or
.Ar private-key-file
refer to binary destination private key files, not public destinations.
Keep these files private.
Only publish addresses, public destinations or signed registration
records.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss autoconf_i2pd
.Bd -literal -offset indent
autoconf_i2pd
.Ed
Interactively select a language, connectivity mode and configuration
options, then write the generated configuration to the chosen file.
Review the output before installation.
The command does not start or reconfigure a running router.
.Ss b33address
.Bd -literal -offset indent
b33address < destination.txt
.Ed
Read a base64 public destination from standard input and print its
blinded
b33 address and the current day's store hash.
The destination must use Ed25519 or RedDSA.
Do not supply a private key serialization.
.Ss b33offlinekeys
.Bd -literal -offset indent
b33offlinekeys output-keys destination-keys [days]
.Ed
Generate per-day offline keys for an encrypted LeaseSet.
The output file must differ from the destination key file.
The destination must use Ed25519 or RedDSA.
The default is 365 days; the accepted range is 1 through 65535.
The output file contains secrets and is intended for the router.
.Ss famtool
.Bd -literal -offset indent
famtool [-v] -g -n family -c certificate -k private-key
famtool [-v] -s -n family -k private-key -i router.keys \
    -f router.info
famtool [-v] -V -c certificate -f router.info
.Ed
Generate a router-family certificate
.Pq Fl g ,
sign RouterInfo family membership
.Pq Fl s ,
or verify membership
.Pq Fl V .
.Fl v
prints progress and
.Fl h
prints help.
The generated PEM private key is unencrypted; keep it offline and
private.
.Ss i2pbase64
.Bd -literal -offset indent
i2pbase64 [-d] [filename]
.Ed
Encode binary input using the I2P base64 alphabet, or decode with
.Fl d .
Read standard input when no filename is given and write standard output.
Decoding accepts ASCII whitespace.
.Fl h
prints help.
Use a separate destination file for redirected output; redirecting onto
the
input file truncates it before this command can read it.
.Ss keygen
.Bd -literal -offset indent
keygen output-file [signature-type]
.Ed
Create a binary destination private key file.
The signature type may be an I2P numeric type or a supported name;
the default is Ed25519, type 7.
Availability of other algorithms depends on the selected crypto library.
The output contains private key material.
.Ss keyinfo
.Bd -literal -offset indent
keyinfo [-v] [-d] [-p] [-b] privatekey.dat
.Ed
Inspect a destination private key file.
The default output is its base32 address.
.Fl d
prints the public destination in base64,
.Fl p
prints the private destination key in base64,
.Fl v
prints identity details, and
.Fl b
prints b33 and offline-key information.
Never publish or retain diagnostic output from
.Fl p .
.Ss offlinekeys
.Bd -literal -offset indent
offlinekeys output-file keys-file [signature-type] [days]
.Ed
Create an offline signature and transient signing keys for a
destination.
The default transient signature is Ed25519 and the default duration is
365 days.
The duration must be from 1 through 3650 days.
The output must not resolve to the input file.
Keep the main destination key offline and protect the generated key
file.
.Ss regaddr
.Bd -literal -offset indent
regaddr private-key-file hostname
.Ed
Write a signed host registration record to standard output.
Submit the signed record, never the private key, to a registration
service.
.Ss regaddr_3ld
.Bd -literal -offset indent
regaddr_3ld step1 subdomain-keys subdomain > step1.txt
regaddr_3ld step2 step1.txt domain-keys domain > step2.txt
regaddr_3ld step3 step2.txt subdomain-keys > record.txt
.Ed
Create a signed third-level subdomain record in three steps.
Each step reads the preceding record when applicable and writes only the
resulting signed record to standard output.
The command has no help option; an invalid invocation prints the step
grammar to standard error.
.Ss regaddralias
.Bd -literal -offset indent
regaddralias old-keys new-keys hostname
.Ed
Write a signed alias record binding the old and new public destinations.
Both input key files are private.
The resulting record includes both signatures and may be published.
.Ss routerinfo
.Bd -literal -offset indent
routerinfo [-6] [-p] [-y] [-f] routerinfo.dat ...
routerinfo [-6] [-y] --firewall=backend [options] \
    routerinfo.dat ...
.Ed
List published transport addresses from one or more RouterInfo files.
.Fl 6
includes IPv6,
.Fl p
includes ports in listing mode, and
.Fl y
selects Yggdrasil addresses when present.
.Fl f
prints legacy iptables output fragments.
.Pp
.Fl -firewall Ns = Ns Ar backend
prints outbound rules for
.Cm iptables ,
.Cm nftables ,
.Cm windows ,
or
.Cm pf .
The command never applies rules or reloads a firewall.
Review the generated output before applying it with the platform's
tools.
The IPv6 and Yggdrasil selections are explicit in firewall mode too.
.Pp
The nftables backend additionally accepts
.Fl -nft-family Ns = Ns Cm inet Ns | Ns Cm ip Ns | Ns Cm ip6 ,
.Fl -nft-table Ns = Ns Ar name ,
and
.Fl -nft-chain Ns = Ns Ar name .
Defaults are
.Cm inet ,
.Cm filter
and
.Cm output .
Those objects must already exist in the firewall configuration.
.Fl h
or
.Fl -help
prints help.
.Ss vain
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vain [options] pattern
.Ed
Generate an Ed25519 destination matching a base32 prefix.
.Fl r
or
.Fl -reg
interprets the pattern as a regular expression.
.Fl t
or
.Fl -threads
sets the worker count from 1 through 1024.
.Fl s
or
.Fl -signature
selects the signature type; only Ed25519 is implemented.
.Fl o
or
.Fl -output
sets the output path prefix.
.Fl m
or
.Fl -multiplymode
continues after a match.
.Fl h
or
.Fl -help
prints help.
Searching may consume substantial CPU.
Generated destination files contain private keys.
.Ss verifyhost
.Bd -literal -offset indent
verifyhost 'host record'
.Ed
Verify a signed record produced by
.Cm regaddr ,
.Cm regaddr_3ld ,
or
.Cm regaddralias .
Pass the complete record as one argument.
A successful invocation prints
.Ql Valid destination ;
rejection diagnostics are written to standard error.
.Ss x25519
.Bd -literal -offset indent
x25519 [--help]
.Ed
Generate an X25519 authentication key pair for encrypted LeaseSets.
Standard output includes both
.Ql PublicKey:
and
.Ql PrivateKey:
in I2P base64.
Share only the public key.
Do not capture the private key in logs or support reports.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success.
Nonzero indicates an invalid invocation, rejected input, unsupported
algorithm, crypto-provider failure or file operation failure.
Specific nonzero values differ between commands; scripts must preserve
the
status of the selected command.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/share/doc/i2pd/docs/tools/
Per-command references, formats, examples and security notes.
The documentation directory may be omitted by an optional-documentation
build configuration; these manual pages belong to the Tools component.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr i2pd 1 ,
.Xr i2pdctl 1 ,
.Xr i2pd-tui 1
